From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu: big buffers in fragmented guest memory attach

What: allow up to 262144 memory entries when the guest attaches a buffer's
backing, instead of 16384. An entry is one contiguous run of guest memory of
any length, so this is a count, not a size: at least 1 GiB even when every
entry is a single 4 KiB page.

Why: Linux gives a buffer's pages as they lie in memory, one entry per
contiguous run. A large texture upload in fragmented guest memory needs more
than 16384 entries: Omarchy's wallpaper (6016x3384, an 81 MB staging buffer)
needed 17867. The attach failed (RESOURCE_ATTACH_BACKING -> ERR_UNSPEC),
virglrenderer then reported the app's GL context as broken ("Illegal
resource", "Illegal command buffer") and ignored everything it sent from then
on. The shell drew nothing: black outputs without wallpaper or bar after
about one boot in six with two displays, until the shell was restarted.
The new bound still limits what the guest can make QEMU allocate (an entry
list of at most 4 MiB).

Upstream: not sent yet (QEMU master still has the 16384 limit).
Test: 20 cold boots + 20 reboots with two outputs, no "nr_entries is too
big" in QEMU's log and no black output (notes: display-bugs track); omacvm
check "GPU contexts" reads QEMU's log for context errors.

diff --git a/hw/display/virtio-gpu.c b/hw/display/virtio-gpu.c
index ba6af7c..aba99f1 100644
--- a/hw/display/virtio-gpu.c
+++ b/hw/display/virtio-gpu.c
@@ -874,6 +874,8 @@ static void virtio_gpu_set_scanout_blob(VirtIOGPU *g,
                               &fb, res, &ss.r, &cmd->error);
 }
 
+#define VIRTIO_GPU_MAX_MAPPING_ENTRIES (1024 * 1024 * 1024 / 4096)
+
 int virtio_gpu_create_mapping_iov(VirtIOGPU *g,
                                   uint32_t nr_entries, uint32_t offset,
                                   struct virtio_gpu_ctrl_command *cmd,
@@ -884,10 +886,19 @@ int virtio_gpu_create_mapping_iov(VirtIOGPU *g,
     size_t esize, s;
     int e, v;
 
-    if (nr_entries > 16384) {
+    /*
+     * OmacVM: one entry per guest memory chunk. Linux hands over a buffer's
+     * pages as they lie, so a big texture's backing in fragmented guest
+     * memory easily needs more than 16384 entries (an 81 MB wallpaper
+     * upload: 17867). The old limit failed the attach, virglrenderer then
+     * dropped the app's whole GL context and it drew nothing again. Allow
+     * 262144 entries: 1 GiB even if each is one 4 KiB page (16384^2 RGBA,
+     * the largest texture); the entry list is then at most 4 MiB.
+     */
+    if (nr_entries > VIRTIO_GPU_MAX_MAPPING_ENTRIES) {
         qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR,
-                      "%s: nr_entries is too big (%d > 16384)\n",
-                      __func__, nr_entries);
+                      "%s: nr_entries is too big (%u > %d)\n",
+                      __func__, nr_entries, VIRTIO_GPU_MAX_MAPPING_ENTRIES);
         return -1;
     }
 
